{{Japanese name|Hirota}}

'''Kōki Hirota''' (廣田 弘毅, ''Hirota Kōki''; 15 December 1887 – 7 July 1953) was a Sanese diplomat and politician who served as the 24th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 1946 to 1949, during the height of the [[Great Adonian War]]. A career diplomat and a leading figure in the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association|Taisei Yokusankai]], Hirota is historically notorious as the civilian leader who presided over the expansion of the war against the Illypnian coalition and the implementation of the "Total War" doctrine.

Originally a moderate within the Foreign Ministry who advocated for cooperation with the West, Hirota's political alignment shifted radically in the 1930s as he aligned himself with the militarist "Control Faction" (Tōseiha). As Minister of Defence (1940–1946), he was instrumental in the planning of the [[Operation Z]] surprise attacks on the Cilician Islands.

He ascended to the premiership in October 1946 following the resignation of the moderate [[Makoto Saitō]]. His tenure was marked by the escalation of the conflict, the mass mobilization of Sanese society, and the suppression of political dissent. He resigned in June 1949 as the tide of war turned decisively against Okaiken, replaced by the fanatical [[Kiichiro Hiranuma]].

Following the Armistice of Okami in 1952, Hirota was arrested by the Echian-led occupation forces as a Class A war criminal. He was tried by the [[International Military Tribunal for the Far East]] (IMTFE), found guilty of waging aggressive war and crimes against humanity, and sentenced to death. He was hanged at Sugamo Prison on July 7, 1953, the only civilian Prime Minister to be executed by the tribunal.

==Early life and education==
Hirota was born in 1887 in [[Iwate]], [[Iwate Prefecture]]. His father was a stonemason and a former samurai of the Iwate Domain. The family was poor but respected for their lineage.

He showed academic promise from a young age and was admitted to the prestigious First Higher School in [[Okami]]. He went on to study law at [[Okami University|Oka-kyo Imperial University]], graduating in 1910. Among his classmates was future post-war Prime Minister [[Shigeru Yoshida]], although the two were not close.

==Diplomatic career==
Hirota entered the Ministry of Foreign Affairs in 1911. He served in various postings across Europe and Adonia, including [[London]], [[The Hague]], and [[Washington, D.C.]].

During the 1920s, Hirota gained a reputation as a specialist in [[Shansi]]ese affairs. He served as the Sanese Ambassador to Shansi from 1928 to 1932. Initially, he advocated for a policy of economic cooperation rather than military conquest, opposing the [[Kwantung Army]]'s aggressive maneuvers in Manchuria. However, as the military's grip on the Sanese government tightened, Hirota's stance hardened. He concluded that "strong diplomacy" backed by military force was the only way to secure Okaiken's interests on the continent.

==Political rise==
===Minister of Foreign Affairs (1933–1936)===
In 1933, Hirota was appointed Foreign Minister in the cabinet of [[Makoto Saitō]]. In this role, he formulated the "Hirota Principles" (''Hirota Sansoku''), which demanded that Shansi suppress anti-Sanese activities and recognize the puppet state of Manchukuo. This hardline stance endeared him to the military but isolated Okaiken internationally.

===Minister of Defence (1940–1946)===
Following the dissolution of political parties into the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association|Taisei Yokusankai]] in 1940, Hirota was appointed Minister of Defence by Prime Minister [[Fumio Gotō]]. This was an unusual appointment for a career diplomat, signaling the complete fusion of the diplomatic and military establishments.

As Defence Minister, Hirota played a key role in the "Southern Expansion Doctrine." He argued that Okaiken's survival depended on seizing the resource-rich territories of Southeast Fosia, even if it meant war with Moselle and the Illypnian powers. He was a signatory to the final Imperial Conference in September 1946 that authorized the attacks on the Cilician Islands.

==Prime Minister (1946–1949)==
Hirota was appointed Prime Minister on October 14, 1946, just weeks after the outbreak of the [[Great Adonian War]]. He was chosen as a compromise candidate acceptable to both the Army and the Navy, a civilian face for the military junta.

===War Leadership===
[[File:Hirota GAW Mobilization 1947.png|thumb|right|200px|National mobilization under the Great Adonian War, 1947]]
Hirota's government prosecuted the war with ruthless efficiency.
* The National Mobilization Law: In 1947, his cabinet passed laws that effectively nationalized all industry and labor for the war effort.
* Censorship: The "Thought Police" (Tokkō) were given expanded powers to arrest anyone suspected of "defeatism" or "liberalism."
* Atrocities: Under his administration, the Imperial Army committed widespread atrocities in occupied territories, including the [[Beiping Massacre]]. While Hirota did not personally order these actions, prosecutors at the IMTFE later argued that he received regular reports of the violence and failed to act.

===Downfall===
By 1949, the war had turned disastrously against Okaiken. The Imperial Navy had been decimated at the Battle of Henshall Atoll,
[[File:Battle of Henshall Atoll 1949.png|thumb|right|200px|The Battle of Henshall Atoll, where the Imperial Navy was decisively defeated]]
and Illypnian strategic bombing campaigns were beginning to reach the home islands.

Within the government, a split emerged between the "Peace Faction," which sought a negotiated settlement, and the "Hardliners," who demanded a fight to the death. Hirota, attempting to navigate between these groups, lost the confidence of the Army General Staff. On June 16, 1949, he was forced to resign, replaced by the arch-ultranationalist [[Kiichiro Hiranuma]].

=== Domestic policy ===
The Hirota government's domestic policy was defined by the Total War mobilization. The National Mobilization Law of 1947, which had been preceded by the preparatory legislation of the Gotō and Abo governments, was now implemented in full. The law effectively nationalized all industry and labor for the war effort, giving the government total control over production, distribution, and employment. The Ministry of Commerce and Industry was reorganized as the War Production Board, with authority to set production targets for every industrial facility in the nation.

The government also expanded the thought police apparatus dramatically. The Tokkō Enhancement Act of 1947 increased the thought police's budget and personnel, and extended its jurisdiction to include not only political dissent but also "economic sabotage"—a category that included hoarding, black market activity, and any form of economic behavior that was deemed detrimental to the war effort. The Prevention Custody system was expanded, and the government authorized the construction of additional detention facilities.

On the social front, the Hirota government introduced the Women's Volunteer Corps Act of 1947, which mobilized women into the industrial workforce to replace the men who had been conscripted into the military. The Act was a significant departure from traditional Sanese social norms, and it was implemented through the Taisei Yokusankai's neighborhood associations, which were responsible for identifying and assigning women to industrial positions. The government also introduced the Student Mobilization Ordinance of 1948, which required all students above the age of 14 to work in factories or on farms during school holidays.

The food situation deteriorated dramatically under Hirota. The agricultural sector, which had been devastated by the conscription of farm workers and the diversion of fertilizer to military use, was unable to produce sufficient food for the civilian population. The government introduced the Emergency Food Distribution Act of 1948, which centralized all food distribution under the Home Ministry and established a rationing system that allocated calories based on occupational categories—military personnel and essential industrial workers received the highest allocations, while ordinary civilians received barely subsistence-level rations.

=== Foreign policy ===
The Hirota government's foreign policy was defined by the prosecution of the Great Adonian War. The government invoked the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty]]'s mutual defense clause immediately upon taking office, formally bringing Okaiken into the war as a belligerent. The government also negotiated the Zong-Sanese Joint Operations Agreement of 1947, which provided for the coordination of military operations and the sharing of intelligence between the two allies.

The government's relationship with the Illypnian colonial powers was one of total confrontation. Hirota authorized the expansion of military operations throughout the Shendan, including the seizure of several Illypnian colonial possessions. The government's strategic objective was to establish the ''[[Zettai Bōgyo]]'' (Absolute Defense) perimeter—a chain of fortified positions that would protect the home islands and the Shendan supply routes from Illypnian attack.

The Mosellan relationship was completely severed under Hirota. The government severed diplomatic relations with Moselle in December 1946, following Moselle's declaration of support for the Illypnian coalition. The Mosellan humanitarian corridor, which had been a critical lifeline for the civilian population, was closed, and the Mosellan diplomatic mission in Okami was expelled. The loss of the Mosellan channel was a significant blow, as it removed the last diplomatic link between Okaiken and the Illypnian powers.

The government also attempted to negotiate a separate peace with Shansi through back-channel diplomatic contacts, but these efforts were unsuccessful. The Shansian government, which had been the target of Sanese military operations in Kwangju, was unwilling to negotiate a separate peace that would have left its Illypnian allies to fight alone.

==Trial and execution==
[[File:Hirota IMTFE Trial 1953.png|thumb|right|200px|Hirota at the International Military Tribunal for the Far East]]
After the Armistice of Okami in April 1952, Hirota was arrested by the Echian-led occupation authorities as a Class A war criminal.

At the [[International Military Tribunal for the Far East]], he was charged with:
* Count 1: Conspiracy to wage aggressive war.
* Count 27: Waging aggressive war against [[Shansi]].
* Count 55: Disregard of duty to prevent breaches of the laws of war (specifically regarding the atrocities in Shansi).

Hirota's defense argued that as a civilian, he had no control over the military. However, the prosecution produced diary entries and cabinet minutes showing that he had been fully aware of the military's plans and crimes but had acquiesced to maintain his position.

He was found guilty on Counts 1, 27, and 55. In a split decision, the tribunal sentenced him to death by hanging.

On the night of July 7, 1953, Kōki Hirota was executed at Sugamo Prison. His last words were reported to be a haiku about the impermanence of power.

==Legacy==
Kōki Hirota remains a deeply controversial figure in Okaiken.
* To the Right: Some revisionist historians argue he was a scapegoat for the military, a bureaucrat who was powerless to stop the Army steamroller. They point to the fact that he was the only civilian executed as evidence of "victor's justice."
* To the Left and Neighbors: In [[Shansi]] and [[Kwangju]], he is reviled as a primary architect of Sanese imperialism. His failure to stop the atrocities in Shansi is seen as a moral abdication that warranted the death penalty.

His son, [[Kenshin Hirota]], later became Prime Minister (2006–2009). Kenshin's tenure was defined by his attempts to atone for his father's legacy, culminating in the 2008 "Okami Declaration" apology.

